WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.358 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can one amount have three different mathematical names?

00:00:04.008 --> 00:00:08.022 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are connecting fractions, decimals and percentages.

00:00:08.242 --> 00:00:16.201 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will use the same whole each time, rename shares in
hundredths, and explain why the notation changes while the
amount stays the same.

00:00:16.851 --> 00:00:23.146 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to connect familiar fractions, decimals
and percentages that represent the same amount.

00:00:23.796 --> 00:00:25.553 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:25.773 --> 00:00:30.200 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can match familiar fractions to equivalent decimals and
percentages.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.472 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use a visual model to explain why the forms are
equivalent.

00:00:34.692 --> 00:00:39.401 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can place equivalent representations at the same position
on a number line.

00:00:40.051 --> 00:00:46.539 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A fraction describes equal parts, a decimal uses place-value
parts, and a percentage uses hundredths.

00:00:46.759 --> 00:00:50.756 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Rename a familiar fraction in hundredths to connect all
three.

00:00:50.976 --> 00:00:53.409 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Always keep the whole unchanged.

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:56.909 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One half of a whole is fifty hundredths.

00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:01.953 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In decimal notation that is zero point five zero, or zero
point five.

00:01:02.603 --> 00:01:05.244 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Fifty hundredths is fifty percent.

00:01:05.464 --> 00:01:10.874 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
All three names identify the midpoint between zero and one,
so the amount has not changed.

00:01:11.524 --> 00:01:14.283 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three quarters is seventy-five hundredths.

00:01:14.503 --> 00:01:18.159 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In decimal notation it is zero point seven five.

00:01:18.809 --> 00:01:22.120 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Seventy-five hundredths is seventy-five percent.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:27.856 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One quarter would be twenty-five hundredths, or zero point
two five, or twenty-five percent.

00:01:28.506 --> 00:01:33.403 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One fifth is how many tenths, and what decimal and
percentage does it represent?

00:01:37.403 --> 00:01:40.533 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One fifth is two tenths, or twenty hundredths.

00:01:40.753 --> 00:01:43.631 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That gives zero point two and twenty percent.

00:01:44.281 --> 00:01:47.394 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the same whole and connect through equal parts.

00:01:47.614 --> 00:01:52.920 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One half, one quarter, three quarters, one tenth and one
fifth are useful benchmarks.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.567 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Equivalent representations locate the same amount on a
number line.
